Hypothesis Review: Metric Computation Is a Material Driver of Model Plan Runtime

Executive Summary

Hypothesis under review

> Reducing the number of metrics will not materially improve Model Plan runtime because the dominant cost is reading, scanning and parsing the underlying CSV data.

Based on the Spark execution evidence and controlled metric-only experiments completed to date, this hypothesis is not supported by the observed behaviour.

CSV scanning and parsing is an unavoidable fixed cost. However, the evidence shows that a significant proportion of execution time occurs after the source data has already been read, within downstream shuffle, grouping, aggregation and metric-processing stages.

More importantly, when the same input dataset and same Spark job profile were used with different metric populations, runtime changed materially:

|Run|Metrics Executed|% of 356 Metrics|Runtime   |Reduction vs 239-Metric Run|
|---|---------------:|---------------:|---------:|--------------------------:|
|M67|239             |~67%            |**4h 13m**|Baseline                   |
|M41|148             |~41%            |**2h 28m**|**~42% faster**            |
|M25|91              |~25%            |**1h 57m**|**~54% faster**            |

The input dataset remained unchanged.

This provides direct evidence that metric workload is a material component of runtime, independently of the fixed source-read cost.

The optimisation direction should therefore include metric rationalisation, specifically identifying and removing unused, duplicated or low-value metrics while preserving required model behaviour.

────────

1. Hypothesis Being Tested

The original position was:

> CSV is row-based and must be scanned and parsed regardless of how many metrics are calculated. Therefore, removing metrics will save relatively little because the dominant cost is the fixed I/O and parsing overhead.

There are two parts to this statement.

The first part is valid:

> **The underlying data still has to be read.**

Reducing the number of metrics does not eliminate the requirement to load the relevant source dataset.

The second part does not follow automatically:

> **Because the data must still be read, metric computation must therefore be a negligible component of total runtime.**

The current Spark evidence demonstrates that this second assumption is not valid for this workload.

────────

2. Full 100% Model Plan Execution Evidence

The 100% base Model Plan provides a useful breakdown of where execution time is actually being spent.

|Stage                          |Approx. Wall-Clock|Key Activity Observed                                                   |
|-------------------------------|-----------------:|------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Stage 0                        |**4.0h**          |`DataReaderRDD`, multiple transformations, preparation for first shuffle|
|Stage 1                        |**3.1h**          |Reads first shuffle, processes/group data, writes second shuffle        |
|Stage 2                        |**5.6h**          |Reads second shuffle and performs extensive downstream processing       |
|**Total major-stage execution**|**~12.7h**        |                                                                        |

Approximately:

• 31% of the main-stage execution is in Stage 0.
• 69% occurs in Stage 1 and Stage 2, after the source-reading stage.

This matters because Stage 0 itself is not purely CSV reading.

────────

3. Stage 0 Is Not Equivalent to “CSV Read Time”

Stage 0 begins from:

DataReaderRDD [0]

This confirms that the source data enters Spark during this stage.

However, the DAG then contains a long sequence of MapPartitionsRDD transformations before the first shuffle boundary.

Stage 0 therefore includes:

CSV/file access -> parsing/deserialisation -> initial transformations -> MapPartitions processing -> preparation for redistribution -> shuffle generation

The stage writes approximately:

• 1,156.9 GiB of shuffle data
• across approximately 1.279 billion records

before Stage 1 begins.

Therefore, even attributing the entire 4 hours of Stage 0 to CSV scanning would substantially overestimate the actual CSV-only cost.

At most, the read/parse cost is contained within that four-hour stage.

It is not equivalent to four hours of pure CSV I/O.

────────

4. The Majority of Runtime Occurs After the Initial Read

Stage 1 begins from:

ShuffledRDD [21]

It is no longer reading the original CSV.

Stage 1 processes:

• 1,108.7 GiB shuffle read
• 1,813.5 GiB shuffle write

with:

|Stage 1 Metric|Result  |
|--------------|-------:|
|Runtime       |~3.1h   |
|Median task   |6.9m    |
|Maximum task  |1.1h    |
|Memory spill  |4.5 GiB |
|Disk spill    |88.2 MiB|

This is distributed processing cost after the input data has already entered the Spark execution pipeline.

────────

5. Stage 2 Shows That the Main Constraint Cannot Be Explained by CSV Alone

Stage 2 begins from:

ShuffledRDD [25]

Again, the original CSV is not being read at this point.

Stage 2 consumes approximately:

1,873.6 GiB of shuffled intermediate data

and then executes a long chain of MapPartitionsRDD operations.

Its performance characteristics are:

|Stage 2 Metric       |Result         |
|---------------------|--------------:|
|Stage runtime        |**5.6h**       |
|Shuffle read         |**1,873.6 GiB**|
|Memory spill         |**203.4 GiB**  |
|Disk spill           |**16.9 GiB**   |
|Median task duration |**6.8m**       |
|Maximum task duration|**4.8h**       |
|Median shuffle/task  |**94.6 MiB**   |
|Maximum shuffle/task |**5.1 GiB**    |

Stage 2 alone consumes more wall-clock time than Stage 0.

A typical task takes approximately 6.8 minutes, while the slowest task runs for approximately 4.8 hours.

Likewise, a typical task receives roughly 95 MiB of shuffle data, while the largest receives approximately 5.1 GiB.

This processing happens after the source data has already been read.

It therefore cannot be explained as CSV scan/parse overhead.

────────

6. Metric-Only Benchmark Provides Direct Evidence

The strongest evidence comes from the metric-only experiments.

All benchmark runs used:

• Same input dataset: ETL_90D_Data_260328_260702
• Same job profile: SK-mig12-MF06
• Same Spark execution environment

The major variable changed was the metric population.

Results

|Run|Metrics Population|Metric Count|Runtime   |
|---|-----------------:|-----------:|---------:|
|M25|~25%              |91          |**1h 57m**|
|M41|~41%              |148         |**2h 28m**|
|M67|~67%              |239         |**4h 13m**|

This behaviour is inconsistent with the assumption that runtime is predominantly controlled by fixed input scanning.

If CSV parsing dominated the execution cost, the three runtimes should remain relatively close because every run incurs the same source-read overhead.

Instead:

• 91 metrics -> 1h 57m
• 148 metrics -> 2h 28m
• 239 metrics -> 4h 13m

Runtime rises materially as the metric workload increases.

────────

7. Metric Composition Also Matters

The earlier 100% Test A and Test B runs reinforce this.

Both tests executed six metric operators against the same underlying data.

However:

|               |Test A     |Test B     |
|---------------|----------:|----------:|
|Stage 0        |~11m       |~11m       |
|Stage 1        |~2.1h      |~49m       |
|Stage 2        |~2.0h      |~1.2h      |
|Approx. overall|**~4h 13m**|**~2h 11m**|

The source/input stage is essentially identical.

The downstream execution is not.

This shows that which metrics are executed materially changes the cost of processing even where the source-read cost remains effectively constant.

This also demonstrates that metric cost is not simply proportional to the number of metrics. Different metric definitions have different computational characteristics.

────────

8. Why Metric Computation Is More Than “A Few Calculations”

The original argument implicitly treats metric execution as:

Read CSV -> calculate several values

That does not accurately describe the workload.

The metric operators contain calculations involving combinations of:

• customer_id
• receiver/account identifiers
• device/IP fields
• session identifiers
• payment type
• channel

with windows including:

• 1 hour
• 7 days
• 15 days
• 30 days
• 60 days
• 90 days

and aggregations such as:

• count
• count distinct
• sum
• average
• minimum / maximum
• standard deviation
• first / last

These operations can require Spark to:

• group records
• redistribute records between executors
• sort data
• maintain aggregation state
• create shuffle files
• transfer data across the network
• hold intermediate structures in memory
• spill intermediate state to disk when memory pressure rises
• re-read spilled data
• merge partial results

Therefore, removing an expensive metric does not simply remove a small arithmetic calculation.

It can potentially reduce the amount of grouping, shuffle, memory utilisation, disk spill and downstream processing generated by the plan.

────────

9. Relationship With the Memory Fraction Finding

This is also consistent with the earlier spark.memory.fraction = 0.6 experiment.

Increasing the memory fraction gave Spark a larger working area for:

• sorting
• aggregation
• grouping
• shuffle processing
• intermediate execution state

This produced approximately a 7-8% performance improvement and improved behaviour under contention.

That experiment showed:

> **Memory pressure and downstream execution processing are material to this workload.**

The metric experiments now show:

> **Metric workload is one of the sources of that downstream processing pressure.**

These findings are complementary.

The memory change improves how efficiently Spark processes the workload.

Metric rationalisation reduces how much workload Spark has to process in the first place.

────────

10. What the Current Evidence Supports

The current evidence supports the following conclusion:

> **CSV scanning and parsing represents a fixed execution cost, but it is not sufficient to explain the total Model Plan runtime. A substantial amount of processing occurs downstream of the initial data-read stage, and controlled metric-only testing demonstrates that reducing and changing the metric population can materially reduce execution time despite the input dataset remaining unchanged.**

The evidence therefore rejects the hypothesis:

> **“Metric reduction cannot materially improve runtime because CSV scanning dominates execution.”**

What has not yet been established is the precise runtime saving that would be achieved by removing unused metrics from the final production-equivalent Model Plan.

That requires a final end-to-end benchmark.

────────

11. Recommended Optimisation Direction

The optimisation strategy should now focus on metric rationalisation, rather than treating the entire metric population as fixed.

The objective should not be to indiscriminately remove metrics.

Metrics should be classified as:

|Classification                           |Action                         |
|-----------------------------------------|-------------------------------|
|Required and valuable                    |Retain                         |
|Unused downstream                        |Candidate for removal          |
|Duplicate / redundant                    |Consolidate or remove          |
|Low-value model feature                  |Data Science review            |
|High computational cost / low model value|Priority optimisation candidate|

The current benchmark page identifies 356 metric definitions across 12 metric operators.

If a broader feature count such as ~731 is used elsewhere, it should be kept separate from the 356 metric-definition count unless confirmed to represent the same population.

────────

12. Final Validation Experiment

The next end-to-end test should compare:

|Test                 |Configuration                                                         |
|---------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Full Baseline**    |Current complete Model Plan                                           |
|**Lean Metrics Plan**|Same Model Plan with a materially reduced, validated metric population|

Both tests must use:

• same 100% source dataset
• same SK-mig12-MF06 profile
• same 20K partition configuration
• same cluster
• same source path
• same output path/type where possible
• no concurrent workload

The comparison should measure:

• overall runtime
• Stage 0 / Stage 1 / Stage 2 runtime
• shuffle read / write
• memory spill
• disk spill
• median / maximum task duration
• throughput

The important expectation is not that CSV scanning disappears.

It will not.

The hypothesis being tested is:

> **Does reducing the metric workload substantially reduce the downstream processing that occurs after the fixed source-read cost?**

The metric-only benchmarks already indicate that it does.

────────

Conclusion

The evidence currently points to two distinct runtime components.

Fixed cost

The Model Plan must read and parse the underlying source data.

Variable cost

The plan must then group, shuffle, aggregate and calculate the configured metrics.

The fixed cost remains regardless of metric count.

However, the variable cost changes substantially depending on the metric workload.

The controlled experiments show that reducing the metric population from 239 to 148 reduced metric-plan runtime by approximately 42%, while reducing it to 91 metrics reduced runtime by approximately 54%, despite using the same source dataset and Spark profile.

Therefore:

> **The presence of unavoidable CSV scan/parse overhead is not a valid reason to dismiss metric rationalisation as a performance optimisation.**

The current evidence indicates that reducing unnecessary metric computation should be a primary optimisation workstream for achieving a lower and more sustainable Model Plan runtime, alongside continued Spark/platform optimisation.